      "sample_index": 2,
      "text": "To determine the remainder when \\( n+2010 \\) is divided by 6, we first note that \\( n \\equiv 1 \\pmod{6} \\). This equivalence means that when \\( n \\) is divided by 6, the quotient is an integer and the remainder is 1. In other words, \\( n \\) can be written as \\( n = 6k + 1 \\) for some integer \\( k \\).\n\nNext, we need to find the remainder when \\( n + 2010 \\) is divided by 6. Substituting the expression for \\( n \\) into \\( n + 2010 \\), we get:\n\\[\nn + 2010 = (6k + 1) + 2010 = 6k + 2011\n\\]\nWe need to find the remainder when \\( 6k + 2011 \\) is divided by 6. Since \\( 6k \\) is a multiple of 6, it is congruent to 0 modulo 6. Therefore, we only need to find the remainder when 2011 is divided by 6. We do this by performing the division:\n\\[\n2011 \\div 6 = 335 \\text{ remainder } 1\n\\]\nThis tells us that \\( 2011 \\equiv 1 \\pmod{6} \\). Thus, we have:\n\\[\n6k + 2011 \\equiv k \\cdot 0 + 1 \\equiv 1 \\pmod{6}\n\\]\nSo, the remainder when \\( n + 2010 \\) is divided by 6 is:\n\\[\n\\boxed{1}\n\\]",
