      "sample_index": 3,
      "text": "Given the condition that \\( n \\) leaves a remainder of 1 when divided by 6, we can express this mathematically as:\n\\[\nn = 6k + 1\n\\]\nwhere \\( k \\) is some integer.\n\nWe need to find the remainder when \\( n + 2010 \\) is divided by 6. First, substitute the expression for \\( n \\):\n\\[\nn + 2010 = (6k + 1) + 2010\n\\]\n\nSimplify the expression inside the parentheses:\n\\[\n6k + 1 + 2010 = 6k + 2011\n\\]\n\nNow, we need to find the remainder when \\( 6k + 2011 \\) is divided by 6. Since 6 is a multiple of 6, any multiple of 6 will have a remainder of 0 when divided by 6. Therefore:\n\\[\n6k \\equiv 0 \\pmod{6}\n\\]\n\nThis simplifies our expression to:\n\\[\n6k + 2011 \\equiv 0 + 2011 \\equiv 2011 \\pmod{6}\n\\]\n\nNext, we need to find the remainder when 2011 is divided by 6. We do this by performing the division:\n\\[\n2011 \\div 6\n\\]\n\nThe quotient \\( q \\) is:\n\\[\nq = \\left\\lfloor \\frac{2011}{6} \\right\\rfloor = 335\n\\]\n\nThen, the remainder \\( r \\) is:\n\\[\n2011 - 335 \\times 6 = 2011 - 2010 = 1\n\\]\n\nThus, the remainder when \\( 2011 \\) is divided by 6 is:\n\\[\n2011 \\equiv 1 \\pmod{6}\n\\]\n\nSo, the remainder when \\( n + 2010 \\) is divided by 6 is:\n\\[\n\\boxed{1}\n\\]",
